        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "B–2 First solution: Suppose that P does not have n distinct roots; then it has a root of multiplicity at least 2, which we may assume is x = 0 without loss of generality. Let  $ x^k $ be the greatest power of x dividing  $ P(x) $, so that  $ P(x) = x^k R(x) $ with  $ R(0) \\neq 0 $; a simple computation yields",
          "block_bbox": [
            234,
            1217,
            1254,
            1507
          ],
          "block_id": 6,
          "block_order": 6,
          "group_id": 6,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
              234.0,
              1217.0
            ],
            [
              1254.0,
              1217.0
            ],
            [
              1254.0,
              1507.0
            ],
            [
              234.0,
              1507.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "display_formula",
          "block_content": " $$ P^{\\prime\\prime}(x)=(k^{2}-k)x^{k-2}R(x)+2kx^{k-1}R^{\\prime}(x)+x^{k}R^{\\prime\\prime}(x). $$ ",
          "block_bbox": [
            296,
            1539,
            1167,
            1600
          ],
          "block_id": 7,
          "block_order": 7,
          "group_id": 7,
          "block_polygon_points": [
