        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "Recall that for any complex polynomial P, the roots of  $ P' $ lie within the convex hull of P. To show this, it suffices to show that if the roots of P lie on one side of a line, say on the positive side of the imaginary axis, then  $ P' $ has no roots on the other side. That follows because if  $ r_{1}, \\ldots, r_{n} $ are the roots of P,",
          "block_bbox": [
            317,
            2327,
            1254,
            2614
          ],
          "block_id": 11,
          "block_order": 11,
          "group_id": 11,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
              317.0,
              2327.0
            ],
            [
              1254.0,
              2327.0
            ],
            [
              1254.0,
              2614.0
            ],
            [
              317.0,
              2614.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "display_formula",
          "block_content": " $$ \\frac{P^{\\prime}(z)}{P(z)}=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{z-r_{i}} $$ ",
          "block_bbox": [
            581,
            2651,
            887,
            2767
          ],
          "block_id": 12,
          "block_order": 12,
          "group_id": 12,
          "block_polygon_points": [
