        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "The above argument also carries through if z lies on the imaginary axis, provided that z is not equal to a root of. Thus we also have that no roots of P lie on the sides of the convex hull of P, unless they are also roots of P. From this we conclude that if r is a root of P which is a vertex of the convex hull of the roots, and which is not also a root of  $ P' $, then f has a single pole at r (as r cannot be a root of  $ P'' $). On the other hand, if r is a root of P which is also a root of  $ P' $, it is a multiple root, and then f has a double pole at r.",
