        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "B−4 (based on work by Daniel Stronger) We make repeated use of the following fact: if $f$ is a differentiable function on all of $\\mathbb{R}$, $\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}f(x)\\geq0$, and $f'(x)>0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, then $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$. (Proof: if $f(y)<0$ for some $x$, then $f(x)<f(y)$ for all $x<y$ since $f'>0$, but then $\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}f(x)\\leq f(y)<0.$)",
          "block_bbox": [
            1326,
            1894,
            2348,
            2189
          ],
          "block_id": 24,
          "block_order": 24,
          "group_id": 24,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
